<main>

    <section class="hero">
        <h1 class="fade-up fade-up-2">Install</h1>
        <p class="tagline fade-up fade-up-3">From zero to "Claude has persistent memory" in one command. Public HTTPS in one more.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>1. Install the Vault</h2>

        <p>Requires <a href="https://bun.sh" target="_blank">Bun</a>. Run this anywhere:</p>

        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>bun add -g @openparachute/cli &amp;&amp; parachute install vault</code>
        </div>

        <p>This installs the Parachute CLI, creates a Vault, and starts it as a background daemon on <code>127.0.0.1:1940</code>. You'll be asked one question: whether to add the Vault as an MCP server in your <code>~/.claude.json</code> (default yes — Claude Code picks it up on the next session).</p>

        <p>Verify:</p>
        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>parachute status</code>
        </div>
        <p>Start a new Claude Code session and your Vault is there — ask Claude to write a note, query your notes, whatever. The MCP tools <code>create-note</code>, <code>query-notes</code>, <code>update-note</code>, and friends are all available.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>2. Make it reachable from the web</h2>

        <p>So far, your Vault is bound to <code>127.0.0.1</code> on port 1940 — only Claude Code on the same machine can see it. To use it from Claude on the web, ChatGPT, your phone, or any MCP-capable tool somewhere else, you need a public HTTPS URL. Two options, both free. Pick based on what you already have:</p>

        <h3>Tailscale Funnel — no domain needed</h3>
        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>parachute expose public</code>
        </div>
        <p>Wraps <a href="https://tailscale.com/kb/1223/funnel" target="_blank">Tailscale Funnel</a>. Your Vault lands at <code>https://&lt;machine&gt;.&lt;tailnet&gt;.ts.net/vault/default</code> — stable across restarts, no custom domain required. If you don't have Tailscale installed, the command tells you where to get it; <code>tailscale up</code> plus enabling Funnel in the <a href="https://login.tailscale.com/admin/acls" target="_blank">Tailscale admin console</a> (Access Controls → add a <code>funnel</code> ACL) is a one-time 5-minute setup.</p>

        <h3>Cloudflare Tunnel — use your own domain</h3>
        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>parachute expose public --cloudflare --domain vault.example.com</code>
        </div>
        <p>Wraps a <a href="https://developers.cloudflare.com/cloudflare-one/connections/connect-networks/" target="_blank">Cloudflare Named Tunnel</a>. Requires <code>cloudflared</code> (<code>brew install cloudflared</code> on macOS) and a domain added to your Cloudflare account (any registrar — Cloudflare just manages DNS). On first run: <code>cloudflared tunnel login</code>, pick the zone, then the command above creates the tunnel, routes DNS, starts the daemon. Your Vault lands at <code>https://vault.example.com/vault/default</code> — your chosen hostname, stable across restarts.</p>

        <p>Want it tailnet-only (your devices, not the public internet)? Use <code>parachute expose tailnet</code> — same URL shape as Tailscale Funnel, just not reachable externally.</p>

        <h3>Authenticate clients</h3>
        <p>The Vault is auth-gated by default — every request to <code>/api/*</code> and <code>/mcp</code> requires a valid token or completed OAuth session. When you put the Vault behind a public URL, you're not <em>adding</em> auth; you're deciding how new clients prove they should have access. Two paths:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>OAuth + TOTP 2FA</strong> — right for human clients (claude.ai, ChatGPT, Notes). One-time setup:
                <div class="code-inline"><code>parachute vault set-password &amp;&amp; parachute vault 2fa enroll</code></div>
                Clients complete a browser handshake the first time they connect; per-client tokens are tracked and revocable.
            </li>
            <li><strong>API tokens</strong> — right for scripts, agents, or services you control end-to-end:
                <div class="code-inline"><code>parachute vault tokens create --scope vault:read</code></div>
                Creates a read-only token. Use <code>--scope vault:write</code> or <code>--scope vault:admin</code> for broader access; no flag at all gives a full-scope token. Paste the token into the client's <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> header. Revocable per token.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>Neither is a prerequisite for the other; either works on its own. Full auth reference in <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-vault/blob/main/docs/auth-model.md" target="_blank">docs/auth-model.md</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>3. Connect from Claude, ChatGPT, or any MCP client</h2>

        <p>Once your Vault has a public URL, add it as a custom connector:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Claude</strong> (web / Desktop / mobile): <a href="https://claude.ai/settings/connectors" target="_blank">claude.ai → Settings → Connectors</a>, click "Add custom connector", paste your Vault URL (e.g. <code>https://...ts.net/vault/default</code>), complete the OAuth handshake.</li>
            <li><strong>ChatGPT</strong> (web / Desktop): <a href="https://chatgpt.com" target="_blank">chatgpt.com</a> → Settings → Connectors → same flow.</li>
            <li><strong>Any other MCP-capable tool</strong>: point it at the same URL. Auth is OAuth by default; API tokens also available via <code>parachute vault tokens create</code> if you prefer a long-lived bearer token.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Running it somewhere other than your laptop</h2>

        <p>Your laptop sleeps. If you want your Vault always on, you have options:</p>

        <p><strong>Wait for Parachute Cloud.</strong> We're working on a hosted version — same architecture, same guarantees, no server to run. <a href="https://openparachute.substack.com" target="_blank">Subscribe for updates</a>.</p>

        <p><strong>Run it on a Mac or Linux box yourself.</strong> Vault is Bun + SQLite. Install Bun, clone the repo, <code>bun src/cli.ts init</code>. On Linux enable the systemd unit in <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-vault/tree/main/deploy" target="_blank">deploy/</a>; on macOS the init command wires up a launchd agent for you. Any VPS works — Render, Railway, Fly.io, Hetzner, DigitalOcean, or a home server. We don't have step-by-step per-provider guides yet; want one? <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-vault/issues" target="_blank">Open an issue</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Port 1940 already in use.</strong> <code>lsof -i :1940</code> to see what's there. Change ports in <code>~/.parachute/vault/.env</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Claude Code doesn't see the MCP.</strong> Start a new Claude Code session (config is loaded at session start). Run <code>/mcp</code> in Claude Code to confirm. If it's missing, <code>parachute-vault mcp-install</code> adds it.</li>
            <li><strong><code>parachute expose public</code> says "funnel not enabled".</strong> Enable it in the <a href="https://login.tailscale.com/admin/acls" target="_blank">Tailscale admin console</a> → Access Controls → add a <code>funnel</code> nodeAttrs rule, and turn on MagicDNS + HTTPS Certificates in DNS.</li>
            <li><strong><code>parachute expose public --cloudflare</code> says cloudflared is not installed.</strong> <code>brew install cloudflared</code> on macOS, <a href="https://pkg.cloudflare.com/install" target="_blank">pkg.cloudflare.com/install</a> on Linux.</li>
            <li><strong>Cloudflare says "not logged in" or the domain isn't in your zones.</strong> Run <code>cloudflared tunnel login</code> (browser flow) after install; add your domain at <a href="https://dash.cloudflare.com" target="_blank">dash.cloudflare.com</a> → Add site if needed.</li>
            <li><strong>Something on your LAN used to reach the Vault but doesn't anymore.</strong> The Vault now binds <code>127.0.0.1</code> by default. If you intentionally expose it via nginx / Caddy / another reverse proxy from a different host, set <code>VAULT_BIND=0.0.0.0</code> in <code>~/.parachute/vault/.env</code> and restart.</li>
            <li><strong>OAuth fails with "No pending flow".</strong> You clicked a home-screen shortcut before completing the handshake. Open the Vault URL in a regular tab and restart the OAuth flow.</li>
            <li><strong><code>InvalidPackageResolution: failed to parse lockfile</code> or <code>FileNotFound: failed opening node_modules/package dir for package</code>.</strong> Known quirk on bun 1.2.x — the package actually installed despite the warning. CLI 0.2.1+ detects this and continues; if you're stuck on an older CLI, <code>bun upgrade</code> to 1.3.x or run the service's init directly (<code>parachute-vault init</code>, <code>parachute-scribe init</code>).</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Found something we missed? <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-vault/issues" target="_blank">Open an issue</a>.</p>
    </section>

